1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,800
This is a close look at
one part of SEAVISTA,

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:07,533
a research project built
at FORTH-ICS and co-funded

3
00:00:07,533 --> 00:00:12,141
by Frontex under the Frontex
Research Grants Programme.

4
00:00:12,141 --> 00:00:17,000
SEAVISTA gives every vessel at sea a
score for how unusual its behaviour is,

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:19,174
updated with each report it sends.

6
00:00:19,174 --> 00:00:24,175
An AI agent investigates what those
scores raise, and briefs the officer.

7
00:00:24,175 --> 00:00:26,833
To do that, it needs
the ledger of the sea.

8
00:00:26,833 --> 00:00:31,257
Each track and each score, now and in
the past, and the models themselves.

9
00:00:31,257 --> 00:00:33,700
The awareness server keeps that ledger.

10
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:36,400
It reaches the agent over MCP,

11
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:41,472
the open standard that AI agents
use to reach tools and data.

12
00:00:41,472 --> 00:00:43,833
When a vessel's score crosses a threshold,

13
00:00:43,833 --> 00:00:47,003
the agent is called in to work
out why it moved that way.

14
00:00:47,003 --> 00:00:49,067
Each case needs different evidence,

15
00:00:49,067 --> 00:00:52,290
so the agent chooses what
to look at, case by case.

16
00:00:52,290 --> 00:00:55,554
The usual way is many
small tools, one step each.

17
00:00:55,554 --> 00:01:00,025
Then every step goes through the agent,
and keeps it busy for no good reason.

18
00:01:00,025 --> 00:01:02,133
It costs accuracy, too.

19
00:01:02,133 --> 00:01:05,667
An agent copying numbers as
text keeps only a few decimals,

20
00:01:05,667 --> 00:01:08,314
and cuts long lists short.

21
00:01:08,314 --> 00:01:10,900
The awareness server
is built the other way.

22
00:01:10,900 --> 00:01:13,929
Its one tool for analysis
takes a whole program.

23
00:01:13,929 --> 00:01:16,200
The agent writes a few lines of Python,

24
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:18,333
in a small vocabulary of query,

25
00:01:18,333 --> 00:01:19,833
forecast, score,

26
00:01:19,833 --> 00:01:21,098
draw and report.

27
00:01:21,098 --> 00:01:23,700
The server runs the
program beside the data,

28
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:26,033
where every number keeps its decimals.

29
00:01:26,033 --> 00:01:31,223
Only what the program reports
comes back, as text and pictures.

30
00:01:31,223 --> 00:01:34,933
An officer can also call the
agent in, with a question.

31
00:01:34,933 --> 00:01:37,346
Which ships met another
vessel at sea today?

32
00:01:37,346 --> 00:01:41,082
The scores rate one vessel at
a time. A meeting takes two.

33
00:01:41,082 --> 00:01:44,424
So the agent writes the
search itself, in one program.

34
00:01:44,424 --> 00:01:47,067
It runs over all the recorded traffic,

35
00:01:47,067 --> 00:01:51,374
and lists each pair of vessels
that stayed close while under way.

36
00:01:51,374 --> 00:01:56,059
To see where the pairs are, the agent
draws them on a map. The first try fails.

37
00:01:56,059 --> 00:01:59,979
The error says what the map needs,
in words the agent can act on.

38
00:01:59,979 --> 00:02:03,718
It gives the map its
bounds, and draws it again.

39
00:02:03,718 --> 00:02:06,967
Half the pairs sit in one
circle, around Gothenburg.

40
00:02:06,967 --> 00:02:10,657
The agent draws that circle up
close, with the ports around it.

41
00:02:10,657 --> 00:02:13,767
The tracks run back and forth
between ferry terminals,

42
00:02:13,767 --> 00:02:15,773
in the city and out to the islands.

43
00:02:15,773 --> 00:02:18,296
The search counted those
ferries as meetings.

44
00:02:18,296 --> 00:02:20,767
The agent sees that in its own picture,

45
00:02:20,767 --> 00:02:24,026
and marks them as ferries in
the list it gives the officer.

46
00:02:24,026 --> 00:02:27,051
It sorts the rest by what
kind of vessel each one is.

47
00:02:27,051 --> 00:02:30,167
Pilot boats come alongside
ships off Gothenburg,

48
00:02:30,167 --> 00:02:32,243
in the Great Belt and in the Sound.

49
00:02:32,243 --> 00:02:35,346
Two rescue boats run side
by side in the Sound.

50
00:02:35,346 --> 00:02:39,833
Pairs that report no vessel type
are ones the agent cannot explain,

51
00:02:39,833 --> 00:02:42,510
so it leaves them to the officer.

52
00:02:42,510 --> 00:02:46,081
Here is one of them, a pilot
boat and a tanker in the Sound.

53
00:02:46,081 --> 00:02:47,467
To check its reading,

54
00:02:47,467 --> 00:02:51,300
the agent draws the pair on a map
of the shipping lanes and the ports,

55
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:54,165
with an arrow for each heading
and the times that matter.

56
00:02:54,165 --> 00:02:57,346
Beside the map, it charts
the distance between the two.

57
00:02:57,346 --> 00:03:02,312
The pilot boat closes on the tanker, stays
beside it at its speed, and turns back.

58
00:03:02,312 --> 00:03:06,194
The agent looks at both
pictures before it answers.

59
00:03:06,194 --> 00:03:09,700
SEAVISTA also sets aside
skills for its agents.

60
00:03:09,700 --> 00:03:13,233
Each skill notes the pitfalls
found while exploring the tools,

61
00:03:13,233 --> 00:03:14,656
and how to avoid them.

62
00:03:14,656 --> 00:03:18,454
Without skills, the agent still
works, because the tools are enough.

63
00:03:18,454 --> 00:03:22,372
Their manual is written from the code,
and their errors say what to fix.

64
00:03:22,372 --> 00:03:25,833
With skills, the agent avoids
the pitfalls already found,

65
00:03:25,833 --> 00:03:27,440
and goes straight to the case.

66
00:03:27,440 --> 00:03:32,604
A new model joins as one more
word, and every program can use it.

67
00:03:32,604 --> 00:03:36,467
Queries are read-only SQL
on the session's own store,

68
00:03:36,467 --> 00:03:38,617
and the models are served by Triton.

69
00:03:38,617 --> 00:03:40,867
The server holds no state of its own,

70
00:03:40,867 --> 00:03:44,859
so more copies of it can
serve more agents at once.

71
00:03:44,859 --> 00:03:47,800
SEAVISTA was built at FORTH-ICS,

72
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:53,367
in its Computational Vision
and Robotics Laboratory.
